#### Article 83 states: Any person who traffics, sells, or kidnaps a child for themselves or for sale, shall be punished with imprisonment for a maximum of 15 (fifteen) years and a minimum of 3 (three) years and a fine of at most Rp. 300,000,000,- (three hundred million rupiah) and at least Rp. 60,000,000,- (sixty million rupiah).

These three Articles are very reformative Articles when compared with the provisions in the Criminal Code, which in articles 281 to 296 regulate provisions, where the heaviest criminal threat is Article 295, namely 12 years. When compared, the Child Protection Law has been quite severe in establishing criminal and fine threats as regulated in Article 81 to Article 83.

The problem is, even though the Law has established heavy threats or legal sanctions, there are still Law Enforcement Officials who have not optimally implemented them, so there is still a gap between law in books and law in action. In cases as regulated in Article 81 to Article 83, Law Enforcement Officials often conduct investigation and inquiry processes that are quite heavy and exhausting for child victims of violence, but in prosecution and judges' verdicts they are still light so they are not in accordance with justice.

Violence against women and children also occurs frequently within the household scope, even though we have had a law for 13 years, namely Law Number 23 of 2004 concerning the Elimination of Domestic Violence (KDRT), but nowadays domestic violence is increasing in number. For the prevention and handling of domestic violence in Musi Rawas Regency, the Office of Women's Empowerment and Child Protection of Musi Rawas Regency has established an Integrated Service Center for the Protection of Women and Children (P2TP2A) of Musi Rawas Regency, based on the Decree of the Regent of Musi Rawas Number 703/KPTS/KPP/2015, namely concerning the Establishment of the Integrated Service Center for Women's Empowerment and Child Protection (P2TP2A) of Musi Rawas Regency. The Integrated Service Center for Women's Empowerment and Child Protection (P2TP2A) serves as a place for reporting, handling, rehabilitation and reintegration of victims of violence. If there is domestic violence or other violence affecting women and children, it must be reported to the P2TP2A, however the existence of P2TP2A is not yet widely known by the community.

Law Number 23 of 2004 concerning the Elimination of Domestic Violence, in essence is to realize a household that is sakinah, mawadah, and warohmah, this can be read from the objectives of eliminating domestic violence, namely: (a) preventing all forms of domestic violence (KDRT); (b) protecting victims of domestic violence, (c) taking action against perpetrators of domestic violence and (d) maintaining the integrity of a harmonious and prosperous household. So that we better understand the prohibition of domestic violence, the important matters of Law Number 23 of 2004 concerning domestic violence will be briefly described as follows: Types of domestic violence, include:
a. physical violence, is an act that results in (1) pain, (2) falling ill, or (3) serious injury;
b. psychological violence, is an act that results in (1) fear, (2) loss of self-confidence, (3) loss of ability to act, (4) feeling of helplessness, (5) and/or severe psychological suffering to a person;
c. sexual violence includes: (1) forced sexual relations committed against a person including a child, residing within the household scope, (2) forced sexual relations against one person within the household scope with another person including a child, for commercial purposes and/or certain purposes;
d. household neglect, includes (1) neglecting a person including a child within the household scope, whereas according to the law applicable to them or because of an agreement or contract they are obliged to provide livelihood, care, or maintenance to that person or child, (2) resulting in economic dependence by limiting and/or prohibiting proper work inside or outside the home so that the victim is under the control of that person.

The rights of domestic violence victims are to receive: (1) protection from family, police, prosecution, court, advocates, social institutions, or other parties either temporarily or based on a court order;

(2) health services in accordance with medical needs; (3) special handling related to victim confidentiality;

4. accompaniment by social workers and legal assistance at every level of the examination process in accordance with the provisions of laws and regulations and (5) spiritual guidance services. All parties both from government elements, Law Enforcement Officials (APH) and NGOs, have become members of the P2TP2A of Musi Rawas Regency.

The obligation to establish a Regional Regulation on the Protection of Women and Children Victims of Violence, is in sync with the provisions of Law Number 23 of 2014 concerning Regional Government. In this Law it is stipulated that the authority to carry out women's empowerment and child protection is delegated to Regional Governments, so it is a very good opportunity for Regional Governments including the Regional Government of Musi Rawas Regency, nowadays to establish Regional Regulations with a gender equality and justice perspective and children's rights.

